## 138. Final Cheat Sheet

### Lines

```text
\boxed{{n\choose2}}
```

segments from \(n\) collinear points.

### Square Grid

```text
\boxed{
1^2+2^2+\cdots+n^2
}
```

or:

```text
\boxed{
\frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}6
}
```

### Rectangular Grid

```text
\boxed{
{m+1\choose2}{n+1\choose2}
}
```

### Polygon Diagonals

```text
\boxed{
\frac{n(n-3)}2
}
```

### Maximum line intersections

```text
\boxed{
{n\choose2}
}
```

### Maximum regions from lines

```text
\boxed{
\frac{n(n+1)}2+1
}
```

---

## 139. Quick Reference Values

#### Square grids

```text
2×2 → 5
3×3 → 14
4×4 → 30
5×5 → 55
```

#### Rectangles

```text
2×2 → 9
3×3 → 36
4×4 → 100
5×5 → 225
```

#### Segments

```text
4 points → 6
5 points → 10
6 points → 15
7 points → 21
8 points → 28
```

---

## 140. Practice Set — Basic

Count the requested figures.

1. A 2×2 square grid — total squares.
2. A 3×3 square grid — total squares.
3. A 4×4 square grid — total squares.
4. A 2×3 grid — total rectangles.
5. A 3×3 grid — total rectangles.
6. Five collinear points — line segments.
7. Six collinear points — line segments.
8. Seven collinear points — line segments.
9. A triangle divided into two triangles by one internal line — total triangles.
10. A square divided into four equal squares — total squares.

---

## 141. Practice Set — Intermediate

11. A 3×3 grid — rectangles excluding squares.
12. A 4×4 grid — rectangles excluding squares.
13. Eight collinear points — total line segments.
14. Six general-position lines — maximum intersections.
15. Five general-position lines — maximum regions.
16. A pentagon — number of diagonals.
17. A hexagon — number of diagonals.
18. A 5×5 grid — total squares.
19. A 4×5 grid — total rectangles.
20. A 2×4 grid — total squares.

---

## 142. Practice Set — Advanced

21. A complex triangle diagram with upward and downward triangles — count by orientation and size.
22. A star made from intersecting lines — count triangles.
23. A nested-square diagram — count all squares.
24. A rectangle divided by vertical and horizontal lines — count all rectangles.
25. A figure containing overlapping triangles — count all unique triangles.
26. A crossed quadrilateral — count all quadrilaterals.
27. A diagram with multiple intersection points — count segments.
28. A symmetric figure — count one half, then validate the center.
29. A triangular mesh — count upward and downward triangles separately.
30. A mixed diagram — count triangles, quadrilaterals and rectangles separately.

---

## 143. Answers — Basic

1. 5
2. 14
3. 30
4. 18
5. 36
6. 10
7. 15
8. 21
9. 3
10. 5

